# Swift Code Patterns

Full conventions: `docs/conventions/CODING_CONVENTIONS.md`

## Project-Specific Patterns

### Settings — always through `Store.shared`, never `UserDefaults`

```swift
// GOOD
Store.shared.targetCode = code
let sc = Store.shared.shortcut(for: "formal")

// BAD — skips persistence and the onChange hook that re-registers hot-keys
UserDefaults.standard.set(code, forKey: "targetLanguageCode")
```

A new setting is a `@Published` property on `Store` whose `didSet` writes to `UserDefaults` and calls `onChange?()` when the menu or hot-keys depend on it. Decode persisted dictionaries by merging over the defaults, so an upgrade never drops a key.

### Main-actor by default

```swift
// GOOD
@MainActor
final class ReaderPanel { /* ... */ }

Task { @MainActor in
    defer { busy = false }
    let result = try await llm.translate(text, toLanguageNamed: language, register: register)
}

// BAD — background queue touching UI state
DispatchQueue.global().async { self.statusItem.button?.image = image }
```

`HotKeyCenter` is the one non-isolated class: it is driven by a Carbon C callback and hops back with `DispatchQueue.main.async` before calling app code. SwiftUI views are `struct`s and static-only namespaces (`KeyDisplay`, `Languages`) need no isolation, so neither belongs in that count. Pure helpers may be `nonisolated static func`. Escaping closures that outlive the call capture `[weak self]`.

### Long-running model calls race a timeout

```swift
try await withThrowingTaskGroup(of: String.self) { group in
    group.addTask { /* real work */ }
    group.addTask { try await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: 20_000_000_000); throw TimeoutError() }
    defer { group.cancelAll() }
    return try await group.next()!
}
```

A stalled request must never wedge the app. Retry transient errors once; never retry a timeout or a cancellation.

### Failure is feedback, not a crash

```swift
// GOOD — transient failure
NSSound.beep(); flashIcon("exclamationmark.bubble", revertAfter: 1.1)

// GOOD — user-actionable failure
notify(reason.message)          // NSAlert with a specific, fixable explanation

// BAD
try! SMAppService.mainApp.register()
```

No `try!`, no `fatalError`. Use `try?` for best-effort side effects, `do`/`catch` + `NSAlert` when the user can fix it. Model framework enums get `@unknown default`.

### Privacy is a hard invariant

No `URLSession`, no sockets, no analytics, no `print`/`os_log` of user text, no third-party packages. The clipboard is snapshotted before a swap and restored after.

### Comments — explain WHY, not WHAT

```swift
// GOOD — decodes a magic number
postCmd(virtualKey: 8)      // 8 = C

// GOOD — records a platform gotcha
// .privateState => our synthetic events ignore physically-held modifier keys
private let source = CGEventSource(stateID: .privateState)

// BAD — narrates the next line
// set the busy icon
setIcon(busy: true)
```

Use `///` doc comments on types and non-obvious methods to state the contract or the reason the thing exists, never to restate its name. Use `// MARK:` to group sections in larger types.

### Formatting

4 spaces, never tabs. Braces on the same line. Wrap around 100–110 columns (a long user-facing string may run over rather than be split awkwardly). Underscore separators in long numeric literals: `20_000_000_000`, `120_000`.

### Types

`final class` for reference types, `struct` for values, `enum` for closed sets and for static-only namespaces (`KeyDisplay`, `Languages`). Singletons are `static let shared` with a `private init()`.

### Files and frameworks

One primary type per file, named after it; a small satellite (a SwiftUI view for its controller) may share the file. Drop new `.swift` files straight into `Sources/EasyWrite/` — SPM globs the directory, so no manifest edit is needed. A new **system framework** does need a `linkerSettings` entry in `Package.swift`.
